Forks\nL173: \nL174: cite94†588 forks L175: \nL176: cite95†Report repository L177: \nL178: main\nL179: \nL180: cite96†Branches cite97†Tags L181: \nL182: [Input: Go to file]\nL183: \nL184: Go to file\nL185: \nL186: Code\nL187: \nL188: Open more actions menu\nL189: \nL190: ## Latest commit\nL191: \nL192: \nL193: \nL194: ## History\nL195: \nL196: 312 Commits\nL197: ## Folders and files\nL198: Name  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\nL199: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL200: cite98†.github | cite98†.github |    |\nL201: cite99†docs | cite99†docs |    |\nL202: cite100†examples | cite100†examples |    |\nL203: cite101†launcher | cite101†launcher |    |\nL204: cite102†tests | cite102†tests |    |\nL205: cite103†themes | cite103†themes |    |\nL206: cite104†tools | cite104†tools |    |\nL207: cite105†.gitignore | cite105†.gitignore |    |\nL208: cite106†CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md | cite106†CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md |    |\nL209: cite107†CONTRIBUTING.md | cite107†CONTRIBUTING.md |    |\nL210: cite108†LICENSE | cite108†LICENSE |    |\nL211: cite109†PROMPT.md | cite109†PROMPT.md |    |\nL212: cite110†README.md | cite110†README.md |    |\nL213: cite111†SHOWCASE.md | cite111†SHOWCASE.md |    |\nL214: [Button: View all files]\nL215: ## Repository files navigation\nL216: \nL217:   *   * cite112†README L218:   * cite112†Code of conduct L219:   * cite112†Contributing L220:   * cite112†License L221: \nL222: More items\nL223: \nL224: cite71†cleanyour.tv†cleanyour.tv · cite109†The prompt · cite113†54 TV lists · cite111†12 built on it · cite114†The tweet†x.com L225: \nL226: Strip the bloatware from any Android TV or Google TV over Wi-Fi, and replace the ad-filled home screen with a clean launcher that looks like an Apple TV. No root, no cable, nothing uninstalled, and a one-line restore if you change your mind.\nL227: ### 54 TVs with tested lists  ·  23 brands  ·  12 languages  ·  280 KB launcher\nL228: \nL229: > Vibe coded my parents' TV this afternoon! It was showing a sponsored ad before you could even get to Netflix. Gave Claude Code the model number, it found the TV on wifi, removed 25 Xiaomi packages and wrote a new launcher. Looks like an Apple TV now lol.\nL230: >\nL231: > cite114†@gathint, 17 October 2026†x.com . The replies are where most of the TV lists here came from. The repo was called tv-vibehack until the 19th; old links work.\nL232: ## How it works\nL233: \nL234: Android TVs have the same debug bridge as Android phones. Switch on USB debugging in the TV's developer options, allow your computer once, and the rest is standard Android tooling:\nL235: \nL236:   1. Back up the list of installed packages and settings.\nL237:   2. Disable the manufacturer's telemetry, ad services, content rows and preloaded junk with `pm disable-user`. Nothing is uninstalled, so a restore script can undo every step.\nL238:   3. Install a small native launcher and make it the default home screen.\nL239: The bloat differs by brand, so the prompt has your coding assistant discover it on your TV and ask before touching anything you might use.\nL240: ## Quick start\nL241: \nL242: You need a computer on the same Wi-Fi as the TV and a coding assistant with shell access (Claude Code or similar).\nL243:  |\nL244: --- | ---\nL245: 1. On the TV  | Settings → Device Preferences → About → press OK on Build seven times → back → Developer options → USB debugging on. If you see a Network or ADB debugging toggle, turn that on too. Keep the TV on.\nL246: 2. On your computer  | Paste cite109†`PROMPT.md` into your assistant and fill in your TV model.\nL247: 3. Follow along  | Accept \"Allow USB debugging?\" on the TV, then answer the checklist: which apps you use, which four go in the dock, whether to keep the voice assistant, Chromecast and updates.\nL248: The assistant backs up, debloats, builds and installs the launcher, and checks each step with screenshots pulled from the TV. With this repo as context it takes about fifteen minutes.\nL249: ## What you get\nL250:   * A pitch-black home with a dock of your four favourite apps in a rounded band, where Apple TV puts it.\nL251:   * A top shelf naming the focused app in large type, with an ambient glow in a colour taken from its artwork.\nL252:   * The rest of your apps in a grid that slides up when you press Down.\nL253:   * One floating glass highlight that springs from tile to tile. The focused tile lifts and shows its name.\nL254:   * Three frosted buttons: Inputs (HDMI ports, AV, Live TV), Wi-Fi or Ethernet with the network name, Settings.\nL255:   * A greeting, a thin clock and the date in cite115†Outfit†fonts.google.com , and a matching Clock screensaver.\nL256:   * Themes that change colours, wallpaper, dock and columns without a rebuild, and module cards that let other apps plug a tile in, only with your say-so.\nL257: And a TV that stops working against you: no sponsored rows, no \"Discover\" nags, no analytics phoning home, no apps you never asked for.\nL258: \nL259:  |\nL260: --- | ---\nL261:  |\nL262: Top shelf and ambient glow  | Inputs: HDMI with connection state, AV, Live TV\nL263:  |\nL264: The grid, one glass highlight, the tile's name  | Clock screensaver, drifts so nothing burns in\nL265:  |\nL266: Aurora theme, pushed with `tools/theme.sh`  | Six columns and a six-app dock, same launcher\nL267: ## Supported TVs\nL268: \nL269: Anything running Android TV or Google TV. Samsung (Tizen) and LG (webOS) run different systems; see cite111†SHOWCASE.md for a webOS port. Fire TV takes the debloat half only, since Fire OS will not hand Home to a third-party launcher. A few China-market TCL sets refuse to install any launcher at all; their lists say so in the header.\nL270: \nL271: Tested lists from contributors, one folder per model in cite113†`examples/` :\nL272: Xiaomi 12 · TCL 11 · Sony 8 · CHiQ 2 · Hisense 2 · Philips 2 · Amazon Fire TV Stick · BenQ · Chromecast with Google TV · Hitachi · Insignia Fire TV · NVIDIA Shield · OnePlus · Panasonic · RCA · Redmi · SANYO · Skyworth · Thomson · Toshiba Fire TV · Vu · Stellamore and XGIMI projectors\nL273: The full list\nL274: TV  | OS\nL275: --- | ---\nL276: Amazon Fire TV Stick 4K, 1st gen 2018 (AFTMM, mantis)  | Fire OS [REDACTED_HOST] / Android 7.1\nL277: BenQ E55-735 (Changhong AI PONT, DVB96BQ)  | Google TV 14\nL278: CHiQ AI PONT (Changhong GTVU65, dangni, MediaTek mt5867)  | Google TV 14\nL279: CHiQ AI PONT (DVB93CQ)  | Android TV 11\nL280: Chromecast with Google TV 4K (sabrina)  | Google TV 14\nL281: Hisense HiSmartTV A4 (xiaoyushan, MStar m7322), India  | Android TV 9\nL282: Hisense SmartTV 4K FFM (huangshan, MediaTek)  | Android TV 10\nL283: Hitachi 65QLEDSM20 (MTC, bangbae, MediaTek m7322), Australia  | Android TV 11\nL284: Insignia 55\" F30 4K Fire TV (NS-55F301NA25, AFTDCT31, duckie)  | Fire OS 7 / Android 9\nL285: NVIDIA SHIELD TV Pro 2019 (P2897, mdarcy)  | Android TV 11\nL286: OnePlus TV (D00, Dubai)  | Google TV 14\nL287: Panasonic 4KTV-JUP (Jupiter_PA, MediaTek)  | Android TV 11\nL288: Philips 50PUS8517/12 (TP Vision, PH1M_EA_9970A, MediaTek MT5895), Europe  | Android TV 11\n"
